  "body": "I am testing open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.refusal_phrase_in. Its documented rule is: \"The first refusal phrase the text contains, typographic quotes read as straight ones, or None.\" Reply A is: \"I am unable to comply. I cannot.\" Reply B is: \"I cannot. I am unable to comply.\" The rule requires the check to return \"I am unable\" for Reply A, because that phrase occurs first in the text, and \"I cannot\" for Reply B, because that phrase occurs first there. The check, however, iterates the refusal_phrases tuple in its fixed order and returns the first phrase from that list that is a substring, so for both replies it returns \"I cannot\", because \"I cannot\" appears earlier in the tuple than \"I am unable\". Thus the check gives the same answer for two replies the rule requires it to tell apart. I would be wrong if the check returns different answers for the two replies, specifically \"I am unable\" for Reply A and \"I cannot\" for Reply B.",
